# Minotaur — the seventh .blob character from scratch, the FIRST authored with
# the `box` primitive, and the first with a SINGLE-SIDED part (`side=r`).
# Reference: docs/dev-notes/refs/minotaur-mesh/minotaur.glb — a Meshy demon
# minotaur brawler: bull head with out-and-up wrapped horns, red eyes/fangs,
# massive traps/pecs/abs, red cables across the shoulders, a heavy V-taper,
# and a MACHINED PROSTHETIC on the character's RIGHT leg (thigh cowl, blocky
# stacked shin plates, ankle pistons, flat boot) against a LEFT flesh leg
# with a splayed beast paw. The mesh is the only reference — the plates are
# PAINTED on the mesh, no modelled greebles, so plate seams are `groove`s
# and panel lines are paint, exactly as the brief prescribes.
#
# AUTHORED AT 1.90 m (scale = 1.90/1.217 = 1.56122 x the mesh's bind
# height). The mesh stands 1.217 m in a WIDE SEMI-CROUCH — knees forward,
# ankles swung back, feet splayed (left ankle x +0.231 vs hip +0.112);
# straight-leg standing height would be ~1.26 mesh units, so 1.90 m authored
# is a hulking ~1.95 m standing — taller than the 1.8 m zombie, a brute.
# Every authored number below = mesh units x 1.56122 unless a comment says
# otherwise. Mesh measurements: headless Blender dumps of the committed glb
# (2026-09-02): joint heads from the Armature, per-bone vertex clouds from
# dominant vertex-group weights (>= 0.35), rho = distance to the bone axis.
#
# THE RIG IS meshy-biped (detectRig; verified against the file: all 24 joint
# heads match the dispatch brief's table to 0.0001). THE .l/.r BONES DIFFER
# in the rig (thigh.r 0.2624 vs .l 0.2479, forearm.r +6.4%, foot.r +10.3%)
# but the grammar's mirror block clones ONE len= per pair, so every paired
# bone here carries the .l/.r MEAN — each side then sits within 5% of its
# own rig length, the DONE-WHEN line. Ring-fit alignment is rigid per bone,
# so the fit sees exactly that residual and no more.
#
# THE RIG-vs-SURFACE OFFSETS (why this skeleton hangs the way it does):
# Meshy buries its Hips joint HIGH in the belly (0.695 mesh) while the
# surface crotch is at 0.548 and the hip sockets at 0.635. Chained from the
# rig's own Hips, measured-length legs overshoot the floor; rooted at the
# surface the spine chain — only 0.330 mesh crotch->neck — dies 0.18 BELOW
# the surface shoulder line (1.004 mesh): this character's traps/neck mass
# is unmapped surface the rig never built joints for. So, bonewalker's
# pattern: the pelvis root sits at 0.871 authored (pelvis tail 0.991 = the
# rig's own socket height, which is also what lands measured-length legs
# with the mesh's crouch geometry exactly on sole 0), the spine rides the
# surface, and the clavicles rise at tilt=32 to put the shoulder balls back
# up on the mesh's shoulder line (authored (0.342, 1.545) vs rig (0.389,
# 1.568) — the deltoid ball covers the rest).
#
# THE PROSTHETIC is authored with `side=r` (the first): the grammar's limb
# prims were forced bilateral, and plates on one leg cannot be mirrored
# pairs. The LEFT leg is flesh; the RIGHT wears `box` plates painted metal
# (the mesh's own metal is a smooth slab with panel lines PAINTED on).
#
# blob:rings is PAINT-BLIND and this character is mostly paint: expect it to
# ask the prosthetic plates to shrink toward the flesh underneath — the
# plates are judged by RENDER (corner crispness at 3/4 yaw), not by rings.
model minotaur-r1
  height 1.90
  stance humanoid

skeleton
  # Root at 0.871: pelvis tail = 0.871 + 0.1204 = 0.991 = rig socket height
  # (0.6347 mesh x 1.5612) — the height from which measured-length legs with
  # the mesh's crouch land the ankle at 0.222 and the soles on 0.
  root pelvis at 0.871 len=0.1204          # rig Hips->Spine02 0.0771 x 1.5612
  # Spine chain: measured lengths, small forward pitches (the rig leans
  # forward: z 0.065 -> 0.083 authored over hips->neck).
  bone spine1 parent=pelvis dir=up pitch=2 len=0.1204   # tail 1.111 = waist
  bone chest  parent=spine1 dir=up pitch=2 len=0.1204   # ribcage
  bone spine2 parent=chest  dir=up pitch=1 len=0.0484   # rig 0.0310: short neck-base segment
  bone neck   parent=spine2 dir=up pitch=0 len=0.1063   # tail 1.386 — rig neck is swallowed by traps
  # LONG ON PURPOSE, bonewalker's pattern: the face block rides skull at=0.45
  # (fixed in face.ts), so len 0.807 puts the cranium centre at
  # 1.387 + 0.45 x 0.840 = 1.765 — clearing the trap shelf (tops ~1.61) so
  # the bull head reads as sitting ON the shoulders (render round 2: at 1.71
  # the head sank into the traps). Unmapped: no ring-fit sees this length.
  bone skull  parent=neck   dir=up pitch=0 len=0.8400

  mirror
    # Clavicle rises at tilt=32 so the shoulder ball lands on the mesh's
    # shoulder line (1.545 authored vs rig arm joint 1.5675; the T-pose line
    # is where the DELT tops out, and at rest the arm hangs below it).
    bone clavicle parent=neck    side=0.087 dir=side tilt=32 len=0.3004
    # Arms rest DOWN-and-out (the mesh T-poses) — a heavy powerlifter spread.
    # Measured mean lengths (see header); .l/.r rig values differ <2%.
    bone upperarm parent=clavicle dir=down tilt=40 pitch=8  len=0.3059
    bone forearm  parent=upperarm dir=down tilt=18 pitch=20 len=0.3501
    # Unmapped (rig ends at one Hand joint) — carries the fist only.
    bone hand     parent=forearm  dir=down tilt=10 pitch=15 len=0.1400
    # Legs keep the mesh's wide semi-crouch: hip->knee out+forward, shin
    # swinging back (rig ankle sits BEHIND the knee: z -0.076 authored).
    # side=0.1755 puts the thigh head at the rig's left socket x (0.1124
    # mesh x 1.5612).
    bone thigh    parent=pelvis side=0.1755 dir=down tilt=13.5 pitch=13  len=0.3984
    bone shin     parent=thigh  dir=down tilt=12 pitch=-22                 len=0.4408
    # The foot bone only positions paw/boot prims (house pattern) — horizontal
    # at ankle height, +z forward.
    bone foot     parent=shin   dir=fwd                                    len=0.2095
  end

body
  # ============================================================
  # TORSO — a heavy V-taper brawler. Measured halfwidths (mesh x 1.5612):
  # hip bowl 0.24-0.28 at y 0.98-1.08, waist ~0.23 at 1.11, ribcage/lats
  # 0.30+ at 1.33, traps rising to the neck; belly wall protrudes to z
  # +0.24 authored, back to -0.25 (the abs are modelled in the mesh).
  # ============================================================
  # Pelvis/hip bowl: wide and deep (mesh Hips cloud slabs: xhalf 0.155-0.177,
  # z -0.11..+0.153 at y 0.63-0.69). Block 3 round 1 (mean 67.1mm): r 0.16 ->
  # 0.1283, wide 1.65 -> 1.392, centre (x -0.018, z -0.085).
  # Block 4 (mean 44.4mm): r 0.1397, deep 1.47 (halfdepth 0.205 = the
  # measured pelvis z -0.11..+0.153 mesh), centre back z -0.064.
  blob torso on pelvis  at=0.50 r=0.1397 wide=1.392 tall=1.30 deep=1.470 blend=0.012 offset=(-0.018,0,-0.114) core
  # Waist -> belly (spine1 0.991-1.111). ROUND 5: deep 1.35 -> 1.210 and
  # wide 1.45 -> 1.490. The relief map's V-taper finding — at the flanks
  # (x -0.23) the mesh runs 0.24 deep at the chest and 0.14 at the belly, a
  # 100mm fall, while this body was NEARLY CONSTANT 0.19-0.23 and slightly
  # deeper at the bottom. An INVERTED taper. Pulling the belly's depth in
  # while keeping its width is what restores it.
  bar  torso on spine1  from=0.00 to=1.50 r=0.155 wide=1.490 deep=1.210 blend=0.012 offset=(0,0,0.030)
  # Abs/ribcage. ROUND 5 — CROSS-SECTION PASS, fitted against a FRONT-WALL
  # RELIEF MAP (ref mesh vs this body, per (x,y) cell; see
  # docs/dev-notes/2026-09-03-torso-relief/notes.md). That map is the only
  # instrument that sees any of this: blob:rings fits a radial AVERAGE,
  # blob:measure scores a SILHOUETTE, and blob:depth reports a band median
  # ACROSS x — all three score a dome and a slab identically.
  #
  # What it showed: every height band was the same arch, most proud at the
  # centreline and behind at the flanks. The mesh is a broad flat-fronted
  # slab; this was a narrow dome. wide 1.40 -> 1.700 (halfwidth 0.210 ->
  # 0.255) and deep 1.70 -> 1.620 carries the mass out to where the mesh
  # actually has it. Mean |diff| over 143 torso cells: 21.6mm -> 15.3mm.
  #
  # NOT the unbounded optimum. A free search wanted wide 2.08 (halfwidth
  # 0.312) and traps at x 0.322, and it rendered as a MUSHROOM — a flat cap with
  # the arms hanging beneath it. The front wall is one view and cannot see
  # that, so the search runs inside bounds set by judgement and `wide` sits
  # pinned at its ceiling. The score still wants more. It cannot have it.
  #
  # A `box` was tried here and REJECTED, by hand and then by the search from
  # every start: an ellipse beats a rounded box on this wall once width is
  # free. A box's corners add ~27% more area than the ellipse of the same
  #half-extents (4ab vs pi*ab), which lands as volume where the mesh has none.
  # The remaining centreline error (+27 to +55mm at y 1.363-1.504) is the
  # STERNUM DIP — the mesh has an 18mm hollow between the pecs at y 1.405,
  # narrowing to a 5mm linea alba at 1.171. That is muscle relief, not
  # cross-section, and it is the next pass.
  bar  torso on chest   from=0.30 to=2.50 r=0.150 wide=1.700 deep=1.430 blend=0.013 offset=(0,0.005,0.010)
  # TRAP SHELF on the neck bone (the rig's spine2/neck is buried under this
  # mass — surface shoulder line 1.5675, traps top to 1.64 authored): fills
  # the neck->deltoid gap so the shoulder balls stop reading as detached
  # spheres (render round 1).
  # ROUND 5: offset x 0.052 -> 0.162, moving the pair OUTBOARD. At 0.052 the
  # two halves (halfwidth 0.361 each) overlapped into one mass that filled
  # the THROAT: the mesh's centreline at y 1.499 is z 0.184, a hollow, and
  # this body had 0.257 there — +73mm, the single worst cell on the map.
  # Traps sit either side of a neck. The unbounded search wanted 0.322,
  # which is out over the deltoids and is half of what made the mushroom.
  blob torso on neck    at=0.90 r=0.190 wide=1.90 tall=1.50 deep=1.30 blend=0.011 both offset=(0.162,0.040,-0.010)

  # ============================================================
  # MUSCLE RELIEF (round 6). Every number below is a target read off
  #   npm run blob:relief -- minotaur --y 0.92:1.51 --x 0.24
  # after the round-5 cross-section pass, which is the ONLY tool that sees
  # any of it (rings averages the ring, measure sees the outline, depth
  # takes a band median ACROSS x — and a pec split is a dip at x=0 between
  # two crowns, which a median over x erases by construction).
  #
  # What the map asked for, in its own numbers:
  #   y 1.363-1.504  centreline +27..+55mm proud, flanks only +9..+31
  #                  -> a RIDGE where the mesh has an 18mm sternum hollow
  #   y 1.222-1.316  BEHIND everywhere, -4..-27mm, worst at x +/-0.09..0.19
  #                  -> the lower-pec/upper-ab shelf is simply missing
  #   y 0.940-1.128  right flank -19..-35mm at x +0.19..0.23
  #                  -> the oblique/hip flare
  # Result: 15.3mm -> 12.8mm mean |diff| over the same 143 cells (12.3mm
  # with wider grooves, deliberately not taken: a 200mm-wide 'line' scores
  # by shaving proud material, which is a depth edit wearing a crease's name). Numbers
  # tuned by bounded coordinate descent against that map, then looked at:
  # NOTHING sits pinned at a bound this time (the cross-section pass had two
  # pinned), so the optimum is interior and judgement is not fighting it.
  # (The +51..+64mm at x -0.23 low down is NOT torso: that column is the
  # prosthetic thigh cowl. Verified against the prim dump.)
  # ============================================================
  # OWNER VERDICT ON THIS WHOLE PASS: "i dont see any muscles just the mass
  # and the normal bumpy texture." Correct, and the numbers below did NOT
  # earn their render. Diagnosed rather than re-tuned — see
  # docs/dev-notes/2026-09-03-torso-relief/notes.md. The short version is
  # that geometry cannot carry muscle in this renderer today: the AO term
  # is a single field tap at 0.06 m, so nothing shallower than ~60mm casts
  # any darkening at all; self-shadowing was deliberately cut; and a crease
  # crisp enough to be sharp is a few pixels wide at gameplay distance while
  # one wide enough to see is a gentle dish with no contrast. A spike adding
  # a second AO tap at 0.015 m made these same grooves visible — modestly,
  # but visibly. That is where the fix lives, not in these numbers.
  #
  # Kept anyway because the MASSES are a measured improvement to the form
  # (15.3mm -> 12.8mm) and the grooves are nearly free and will read the day
  # a cavity term lands.
  #
  # GROOVE WIDTHS BELOW LOOK ABSURDLY SMALL. They are not. `depth` is metres
  # but `width` is compared against sdPrimitive's SCALED distance, so the
  # channel's real half-width is `width / min(wide, tall, deep)` — and a
  # groove plate is FLAT by definition, which is what multiplies it. At
  # tall=0.05, width=0.010 is a 200mm band: measured here as a 40mm trench
  # down the whole body, not a line. width=0.0010 is the 20mm line intended.
  # Nobody could have known this before 2026-09-03: every groove in every
  # character was a silent no-op (placePrims dropped depth/width while
  # keeping op), so no authored groove number had ever been seen.
  #
  # PECS. A pair, not one mass, so the centreline can fall BETWEEN them —
  # that is the whole construction. The chest bar's depth comes down to pay
  # for them (round 5 left it carrying the centre alone, which is what made
  # the ridge). neck at=0.72 -> y 1.397, the mesh's pec crown height.
  blob torso on neck   at=0.72 offset=(0.098,0,0.128) r=0.108 wide=1.30 tall=1.15 deep=0.62 blend=0.006 both
  # Lower-pec shelf: the line that makes a pec read as a PLATE rather than a
  # bulge. neck at=0.30 -> y 1.352.
  groove torso on neck at=0.30 offset=(0.090,0,0.150) r=0.115 wide=1.39 tall=0.05 depth=0.0150 width=0.00035 both
  # Sternum. The mesh's own hollow is 18mm at y 1.405 (relief --abs: z 0.235
  # at x 0 against 0.255 at x +/-0.05). The pair above does most of it; this
  # cuts the remainder.
  groove torso on neck at=0.60 offset=(0,0,0.170) r=0.100 wide=0.05 tall=1.50 depth=0.0220 width=0.0003
  # Upper-ab / lower-chest mass. chest from=0.85 to=1.75 -> y 1.219-1.328,
  # exactly the band the map reports 13-25mm behind.
  bar  torso on chest  from=0.85 to=1.75 r=0.150 wide=1.79 deep=1.66 blend=0.010 offset=(0,0,0.020)
  # Linea alba: 5mm at y 1.171, shallower than the sternum by measurement,
  # not by taste.
  groove torso on spine1 at=1.10 offset=(0,0,0.190) r=0.090 wide=0.05 tall=1.60 depth=0.0120 width=0.00025
  # Ab cross-lines.
  groove torso on spine1 at=1.35 offset=(0,0,0.185) r=0.130 wide=1.25 tall=0.05 depth=0.0130 width=0.00030
  groove torso on spine1 at=0.95 offset=(0,0,0.185) r=0.130 wide=1.25 tall=0.05 depth=0.0130 width=0.00030
  # Obliques / hip flare: the map's -19..-35mm at the right flank low down.
  blob torso on spine1 at=0.10 offset=(0.161,0,0.070) r=0.101 wide=0.95 tall=1.39 deep=1.10 blend=0.008 both

  # Neck: nearly invisible (mesh neck cloud is a 100-vert stub, rho ~0.03
  # mesh); a short bar swallowed by the traps, fusing head->torso.
  bar  head on neck from=-0.30 to=1.20 r=0.060 deep=1.20 blend=0.006

  # ============================================================
  # HORNS — the mesh's second-biggest silhouette feature after the leg.
  # Measured off the Head cloud: bases at the cranium's top-sides (x +/-0.075
  # mesh, y 1.06-1.10), sweeping OUT to xhalf 0.165 mesh at y 1.13-1.19 with
  # the tips curling up and slightly forward; the outer half is wrapped in
  # near-black bands (texture median there sRGB ~(30,25,25)). Two prims per
  # horn: a skin-covered base blob, then the wrapped sweep.
  # ============================================================
  # Base: skin-covered horn root at the cranium's TOP-sides. Round 4: at=0.375
  # put the roots at y 1.702, which is 34% up a cranium spanning 1.571-1.959 —
  # ear height, so the horns read as tusks off the jaw hinge. at=0.58 ->
  # y 1.873, which is 78% up: the temple/crown corner a bull's horns spring
  # from. The cranium's own x-halfwidth there is 0.104 (0.125 x sqrt(1 -
  # ((1.873-1.765)/0.194)^2)), so a root centred at x 0.098 with r 0.055 sits
  # ON the surface and proud of it by 0.05 — a root bump, not a growth.
  blob head on skull at=0.58 offset=(0.098,0.0,0.020) r=0.055 wide=1.0 tall=0.85 deep=1.0 blend=0.005 both color=6e4426
  # Wrapped sweep: one bent tapered bar. Raised with the root (round 4) — the
  # old tips topped out at y 1.849, BELOW the cranium's own crown at 1.959, so
  # the head was taller than its horns and they vanished into the silhouette.
  # Now: root (0.098, 1.873, 0.029) -> tip (0.260, 2.020, 0.024), clearing the
  # crown by 0.06 and reading against the sky from every yaw. Endpoint arith:
  # b = boneAt(to) + offset + tip, boneAt(t).y = 1.386 + 0.84t.
  # `bend` is the control point's displacement from the MIDPOINT, so
  # (+0.048, -0.042, 0) pulls the curve OUT and DOWN of the chord — the horn
  # leaves the skull sideways and only hooks up at the tip, which is the bull
  # silhouette. A straight chord here reads as a spike.
  # AGAINST THE REFERENCE, honestly: the mesh's horn tips reach the model's own
  # top, 1.217 mesh = 1.900 authored — i.e. in the mesh the HORNS are the
  # topmost feature. Here they were not: an earlier round enlarged the cranium
  # to stop the head sinking into the traps, which put the crown at 1.959,
  # 0.11 ABOVE the old tips. So part of this is a real correction (restore the
  # mesh's own ordering) and part is an art call (the tips now sit at 2.021,
  # 0.12 above the mesh-equivalent 1.900) — a taller horn than the reference
  # carries, chosen for the read at gameplay distance. `blob:depth` scores
  # this as a regression in the two crown bands and it is right to; the
  # deviation is deliberate and this comment is where it is recorded.
  # topY is now 2.033, above the declared `height 1.90`. Fine — `height` is
  # documentation, nothing scales by it (grep: only head-profile.ts reads it).
  bar  head on skull from=0.575 to=0.70 offset=(0.098,0.0,0.020) tip=(0.162,0.046,-0.005) bend=(0.048,-0.042,0.0) r=0.050 r2=0.013 blend=0.004 chamfer both color=221a1a gloss=0.45

  # ============================================================
  # ARMS — huge (mesh upperarm rho p50 0.085-0.096 = 0.13-0.15 authored,
  # forearm tapering 0.11 -> 0.075). Deltoid ball at the clavicle tail is the
  # arm's core (house pattern — the fuse probe must not pick an accessory).
  # ============================================================
  blob arm on clavicle at=1.00 r=0.135 blend=0.008 mirror core
  # Round 2: the .l and .r clouds seesaw around the shared line (the mesh's
  # arms are NOT mirror-symmetric) — the honest target is the mean of both
  # sides' wants: r 0.109, r2 0.112, wide 1.0, offset back to (0,0,0.004).
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.00 to=0.95 r=0.1090 r2=0.1120 wide=1.000 blend=0.006 mirror offset=(0,0.001,0.004)
  # Block 12 round 2: minor converges (r 0.110, r2 0.0793, deep 1.078).
  bar  arm on forearm  from=0.00 to=0.90 r=0.1100 r2=0.0793 deep=1.078 tall=1.131 blend=0.006 mirror offset=(0,-0.001,-0.008)
  # Fist: one deep mitt (mesh hand cloud rho p50 0.055-0.09 -> 0.085-0.14;
  # a fist, not a spread hand — no finger attribution exists in the rig).
  blob arm on hand     at=0.45 r=0.085 wide=0.95 tall=1.10 deep=1.25 blend=0.005 mirror

  # ============================================================
  # LEFT+RIGHT FLESH LEG — authored mirrored (the grammar's bilateral cage);
  # the RIGHT side's surface is then REPLACED by the prosthetic plates below,
  # which is exactly the mesh's own construction: metal over a stump.
  # Measured flesh radii (LEFT cloud, mesh p50 -> authored): thigh mid 0.129
  # -> 0.20 (a muscled thigh; the bar's taper carries hip->knee), calf
  # 0.079-0.091 -> 0.12-0.14 with the bulge back, ankle ~0.09 -> 0.14.
  # ============================================================
  blob leg on thigh at=0.02 r=0.160 blend=0.008 mirror core
  # Block 9 round 1 (mean 26.0mm): r 0.155 -> 0.1171, r2 0.135 -> 0.1669
  # (thinner shaft, knee-end mass — the crouch fans the cloud), centre back
  # z -0.072 (hamstring belly hangs behind the axis).
  bar  leg on thigh from=0.05 to=0.92 r=0.1171 r2=0.1669 wide=1.02 blend=0.007 mirror offset=(0,-0.021,-0.052)
  blob leg on shin  at=0.02 r=0.125 deep=0.95 blend=0.007 mirror
  # Block 11 (mean 15.8mm): r 0.1082, r2 0.1155, deep 1.216 (81% column
  # share), centre back 0.013. The tall 0.545 suggestion (5% share) held off —
  # an effectively unmeasured column.
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.08 to=0.60 r=0.1082 r2=0.1155 deep=1.216 tall=1.0 blend=0.007 mirror offset=(0,-0.002,-0.022)
  # Block 10 (mean 22.4mm): the fit wants this bar to GROW toward the ankle
  # (r2 0.1317 — the mesh ankle is thick, rho ~0.09 mesh = 0.14 authored) and
  # thin at the top where the calf owns the surface (r 0.0369).
  # Block 7 round 2: r 0.0304, centre back (z -0.060 total).
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.60 to=0.98 r=0.0304 r2=0.1317 blend=0.006 mirror offset=(0,0.009,-0.060)
  # Beast paw: splayed (mesh left paw x 0.205..0.521 authored — 0.32 wide!),
  # short and deep, toes forward. Block 11: r 0.0926, centre up 0.046, toe
  # end reaches further (taper REFUSED — uniform r, longer body).
  blob leg on foot  at=0.30 offset=(0.010,-0.054,0.095) r=0.0926 wide=1.35 tall=1.07 deep=1.45 blend=0.006 mirror
  blob leg on foot  at=0.30 offset=(0.085,-0.135,0.130) tip=(0.020,-0.040,0.055) r=0.030 r2=0.008 blend=0.003 mirror color=3a2417
  blob leg on foot  at=0.30 offset=(-0.070,-0.130,0.125) tip=(-0.015,-0.040,0.055) r=0.030 r2=0.008 blend=0.003 mirror color=3a2417

  # ============================================================
  # THE PROSTHETIC — RIGHT leg only (side=r; the first single-sided prims).
  # The mesh's metal is a smooth slab with panel lines PAINTED on — no
  # modelled bolts — so: `box` plates at round=0.05-0.12 (machined), metal
  # paint + gloss for the read, grooves for seams (added after first render),
  # ROUND 4 — POLISHED, not painted. The plates read as matte grey card at
  # gloss 0.70. `gloss` was then the only metal lever the shader had, and it
  # drives four terms at once (march.wgsl.ts): the specular exponent
  # mix(mix(128,4,roughness), 220, gloss), the specular weight
  # mix(surfCfg.x, 1.5, gloss), the fresnel rim mix(1, 2.5, gloss), and `wet`
  # toward 1. At 0.70 all four sit mid-scale, which is exactly "plastic".
  # At 0.90-0.95 the highlight goes tight and hot and the rim lights up.
  # The albedo comes DOWN with it (848a91 -> 6a7078, roughly -35% linear):
  # metal's diffuse is dark and its brightness is the highlight — a light
  # albedo plus a hot spec reads as white plastic, which is what round 3 was.
  # ROUND 5 (hard-surface task 2) — the bare word `metal` on each plate:
  # the shader now drops the diffuse family to a 0.25 floor and tints the
  # specular AND fresnel rim by this paint, which is what was still missing
  # from the plastic read. `metal` implies gloss's noise suppression on its
  # own. STILL a paint approximation, not a metallic BRDF — there is no
  # environment map; this is the honest approximation of metalness.
  # one cable run as a plain bent capsule. Mesh .r radii: thigh cowl p50
  # 0.104-0.114 UNIFORM (a slab, not a taper) = 0.16-0.18 authored, p75 to
  # 0.17 (0.26 authored — the cowl's outer flare); shin plates 0.110-0.131
  # -> 0.17-0.20. Judged by RENDER (corner at 3/4 yaw), NOT by rings —
  # rings sees only the on-axis extent and will ask these to shrink.
  # Offsets are authored in WORLD axes straight onto the .r bones (x
  # negative = outward on the right).
  # ============================================================
  # Thigh cowl: one slab hip->mid-thigh, proud of the flesh on the outer face.
  # Render round 1: the flesh hip ball poked out above the cowl — extended
  # down to the bone head and thickened. Paint darkened (9aa0a6 read white).
  bar  leg on thigh from=-0.05 to=0.68 offset=(-0.033,0.008,0.012) r=0.162 wide=1.28 tall=1.20 deep=1.18 box round=0.10 color=6a7078 metal gloss=0.95 blend=0.010 side=r
  # Knee block: the mesh's dark square knee panel.
  blob leg on shin  at=0.04 offset=(-0.010,-0.010,0.015) r=0.135 wide=1.15 tall=1.10 deep=1.10 box round=0.18 color=4e545c metal gloss=0.93 blend=0.009 side=r
  # Stacked shin greave: two OVERLAPPING slabs so the silhouette steps without
  # gapping; deepened per rings' deep-hint (the mesh prosthetic is deeper
  # front-to-back than a first pass).
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.06 to=0.55 offset=(-0.024,0.005,0.018) r=0.128 wide=1.25 tall=1.15 deep=1.35 box round=0.07 color=6a7078 metal gloss=0.95 blend=0.008 side=r
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.50 to=0.92 offset=(-0.018,0.000,0.000) r=0.115 wide=1.20 tall=1.10 deep=1.45 box round=0.07 color=5d636b metal gloss=0.94 blend=0.007 side=r
  # Flat boot: deep slab, top overlapping the greave's bottom (the ankle slot
  # render round 3), sole planted (bottom just under y 0).
  bar  leg on foot  from=0.10 to=0.70 offset=(-0.010,-0.090,0.045) r=0.100 wide=1.25 tall=1.50 deep=1.60 box round=0.10 color=44494f metal gloss=0.90 blend=0.008 side=r
  # Red cable run down the greave's outer FACE (rides the plate surface:
  # plate outer face ~0.194 from the axis, cable at 0.194+0.016).
  bar  leg on shin  from=0.12 to=0.52 offset=(-0.210,0.020,-0.020) bend=(-0.020,0.020,0.010) r=0.016 r2=0.012 blend=0.004 color=cc2a1a gloss=0.85 side=r
  # Panel seams: the mesh's metal has lines PAINTED on; a groove per slab
  # edge reads as the milled joint (flat axis across the line's direction:
  # tall small = horizontal seam).
  groove leg on shin at=0.30 offset=(-0.150,0.0,0.145) r=0.10 wide=1.10 tall=0.06 depth=0.004 width=0.006 side=r
  groove leg on thigh at=0.30 offset=(-0.185,0.0,0.150) r=0.11 wide=1.15 tall=0.06 depth=0.004 width=0.006 side=r

  # Red cables across the shoulders/upper arms (mesh: paired cables from the
  # clavicle region out along the deltoid, both sides) — ridden on the arm's
  # back surface (r 0.109 + cable 0.014 -> z -0.128).
  bar  arm on upperarm from=0.05 to=0.75 offset=(0,-0.045,-0.128) bend=(0,0.045,0.030) r=0.014 r2=0.011 blend=0.004 mirror color=cc2a1a gloss=0.85

# ============================================================
# FACE — baked decal (the mesh has a painted face: red eyes, fangs, open
# mouth). The face block's cranium: mesh head slabs give halfwidth ~0.066
# mesh (0.103 authored) below the horn bases, head z -0.055..+0.197
# (muzzle forward), chin ~0.99 mesh = 1.546 authored, crown 1.217 = 1.900.
# headRadius 0.100 x the scales below: half (0.100, 0.180, 0.155) about
# centre (0, 1.710, 0.009).
# ============================================================
face
  # Enlarged (render round 2: the head sank into the traps — the mesh's head
  # with jaw/muzzle is a BIG mass: head cloud halfwidth 0.066 mesh cranium
  # but 0.103-0.165 with muzzle+horns). Semi-axes (0.125, 0.194, 0.188).
  headRadius 0.125
  headWidth  1.00
  headHeight 1.55
  headDepth  1.50
  jawWidth   0.95
  jawHeight  1.00
  jawDrop    0.130
  jawJut     0.110
  noseLength 0.030
  noseWidth  0.80
  noseDrop   0.030
  browHeavy  0.016
  browRise   0.040
  headBlend  0.005

# Face decal: `npm run blob:face-bake -- minotaur` baked the mesh's own head
# (top 16.5%: red eyes, snarl, fangs; the horn tips widen the box so the FACE
# occupies only the centre ~28% of uv.x). The PNG is CROPPED to that band
# (uv.x 0.34..0.66, uv.y 0.28..0.60, alpha zeroed elsewhere) so the bake's
# horn/trap imagery can never wrap past the cranium's silhouette. Projection
# aimed at TWO features in head space of the fattest head prim (the cranium,
# centre (0, 1.764, 0.009), semi-axes 0.125 x 0.194 x 0.188): the image's
# EYE row (uv.y 0.50) at authored y 1.73 -> hs.y -0.176, and the image's
# FANG row (uv.y 0.395) at authored y 1.665 -> hs.y -0.510. Solve:
# scaleY 0.314, centreY 0.555. scaleX 0.139 puts the bake's eye gap
# (duv.x 0.078) on the mesh's own +/-0.035 eye spacing (hs.x +/-0.28).
sheet
  image       minotaur-face.png
  decal       1
  projScaleX  0.130
  projScaleY  0.314
  projCentreX 0.5
  projCentreY 0.555

# Palette. ROUND 4 — the mesh's own texture is a warm mid brown and the
# measured values below rendered as a dark chocolate mass with no read at all
# under the lab's key. Retargeted to the ZOMBIE's flesh, which is the
# `henenlotter-latex` preset (material.ts) and is what every character gets by
# default: baseColor 0.82 0.44 0.46, a foam-latex pink under a hard key.
# So the numbers here are now the PRESET's, deviating only where this
# character needs it:
#   - baseColor lifted 0.82 -> 0.86 to pay for the mottle. material.ts is
#     explicit that average albedo lands near mix(base, mottle, amp x 0.5), so
#     with amp 0.22 and mottle 0.62 0.28 0.30 the body averages
#     (0.834, 0.449, 0.460) — the preset's colour, arrived at WITH the mottle
#     rather than before it.
#   - the mottle stays (this is hide, not skin) but moves into the pink
#     family; the old dusty grey-gold 0.16 0.10 0.06 was half of what made
#     the body read brown.
#   - surfaceNoiseAmp 0.09 and silhouetteNoiseAmp 0.014. ROUND 6 halved the
#     noise and the mottle (0.16 -> 0.09, amp 0.22 -> 0.11): with muscle
#     relief finally in the geometry, the mottle's blotches sit at roughly
#     the same spatial frequency as a pec and were the LOUDER signal. Both
#     move toward the preset, which carries mottleAmp 0 — so this is closer
#     to the zombie, not further from it. Honest scale of the win: modest.
#     The mottle is still the loudest thing on the chest at close range.
# The measured brown (baseColor 0.115 0.055 0.025, mottle 0.16 0.10 0.06) is
# the REFERENCE's colour, kept in this comment and in git — it is not the
# colour this character should be in this game.
palette
  baseColor     0.86 0.47 0.48
  deepColor     0.74 0.06 0.10
  charColor     0.10 0.07 0.08
  specIntensity 0.95
  specRoughness 0.16
  fresnelBoost  0.75
  translucency  0.45
  surfaceNoiseAmp    0.09
  silhouetteNoiseAmp 0.014
  wetness       0.85
  mottleAmp     0.11
  mottleScale   1.6
  mottleColor   0.62 0.28 0.30
